<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usman dan Fodio</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as dan Fodio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Usman dan Fodio was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> Islamic scholar and reformer who led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms beginning in 1804 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. His movement created one of the largest states in nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and inspired other reform movements across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. The jihad also threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>, contributing to its decline.</p></div>
